## 6. Root Cause

The selected vulnerable Dockerfile explicitly copied the synthetic credential file into the production image.

The file was available in the build context, and the `.dockerignore` entry for `.env` did not exclude it.

The issue originated in the image build. Application health checks and container restarts could not correct that build defect.

## 7. Containment

After collecting the available evidence, I stopped the backend:

```bash
docker stop warroom-local-backend
```

I confirmed its status:

```bash
docker ps -a --filter name=warroom-local-backend
```

The container reported:

```text
Exited (143)
```

This was consistent with termination following `docker stop`.

The action interrupted the simulation API and preserved the container until replacement.

No credential rotation was necessary for the nonfunctional training canary. A real exposed credential would require revocation or rotation as well as image remediation.

