## 9. Verification

### 9.1 Replacement Image Identity

The replacement container reported:

```text
sha256:d2143e5457140c205b76b90df83d9eb2e6ec9a46d6cb21b6d0cb65c0836c2410
```

This differed from the original affected image identity.

These are identities reported by local Docker. No registry publication or registry digest verification was performed for the simulation images.

### 9.2 Final Filesystem

The replacement check returned:

```text
PASS: canary file absent
```

### 9.3 Image History

The replacement image history showed the expected scoped copies and no instruction copying `training-canary.env`.

Older timestamps reflected reused build layers. The `<missing>` entries represented historical layers without separately available image IDs.

History supported the expected build configuration but was not treated as sufficient proof of clean layer contents.

### 9.4 Exact Deployed-image Layer Scan

I exported the exact deployed replacement image and scanned the regular-file contents of every exported layer for the full synthetic credential.

The scan handled compressed layer archives and stopped if export or parsing failed.

The result was:

```text
Checking deployed image: sha256:d2143e5457140c205b76b90df83d9eb2e6ec9a46d6cb21b6d0cb65c0836c2410
PASS: full synthetic credential absent from all 10 image layers
```

This established absence of the specific training credential from the checked layer contents. It did not certify that the image contained no other secrets or vulnerabilities.

### 9.5 Application Recovery

I restarted the frontend and checked the API:

```bash
